window.EDITORIAL = {"id":"ed_2026-09-26","publishDate":"2026-09-26","status":"published","title":"Deconstructing the Bean","dek":"Science, Resurgent Terroirs, and the Post-Dogma Era of Coffee","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://www.baristahustle.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Exploding-Coffee-Bean-03.jpg","heroCredit":"The End of First Crack?","heroSource":"Barista Hustle","heroSourceUrl":"https://www.baristahustle.com/the-end-of-first-crack/","wordCount":334,"paragraphs":["As sensory research challenges long-held roasting tenets, the Fourth Wave looks to emerging origins and high-precision home extraction to redefine the perfect cup.","The transition from Third to Fourth Wave coffee is defined by rigorous scientific inquiry quietly replacing artisanal folklore. This week, we are witnessing a profound deconstruction of foundational coffee dogmas. Groundbreaking discourse is challenging the sacred status of the \"first crack\"—long heralded as the critical, make-or-break pivot point in roast profiling—suggesting our obsessive micro-adjustments during this phase may be far less consequential than previously believed. Furthermore, our very understanding of sensory perception is being upended. Recent research indicates that the organic acids we so confidently claim to taste in a bright, complex pour-over are present in concentrations so low they border on the undetectable. We are no longer just brewing coffee; we are beginning to decode the cognitive illusions of flavor.","This scientific awakening coincides with a necessary geographic expansion of the specialty coffee map. As climate realities shift, the industry is looking beyond traditional equatorial strongholds. We are tracking the compelling resurgence of Sri Lanka—once a global coffee titan in the 1860s before being decimated by leaf rust—now strategically positioning itself to reclaim its heritage through meticulously processed specialty lots. Concurrently, the high-altitude, untapped microclimates of Nepal are drawing serious attention, promising entirely new terroirs and flavor profiles for the discerning palate.","Translating these macro-shifts from the farm and the laboratory to the cup requires both culinary innovation and precise equipment. On the hospitality front, institutions like The Caffè by Mr. Espresso are actively blurring the lines between gastronomy and cafe culture with highly curated seasonal pop-ups. Meanwhile, the democratization of precision extraction continues at home, with newly minted gear guides highlighting the increasingly sophisticated espresso technology now available for under $250.","Ultimately, the Fourth Wave is characterized by this relentless pursuit of clarity. By stripping away outdated sensory myths and expanding our agronomic borders, we continue to elevate coffee from a romanticized craft to a precise, relentlessly evolving science."],"body":"As sensory research challenges long-held roasting tenets, the Fourth Wave looks to emerging origins and high-precision home extraction to redefine the perfect cup.\n\nThe transition from Third to Fourth Wave coffee is defined by rigorous scientific inquiry quietly replacing artisanal folklore. This week, we are witnessing a profound deconstruction of foundational coffee dogmas. Groundbreaking discourse is challenging the sacred status of the \"first crack\"—long heralded as the critical, make-or-break pivot point in roast profiling—suggesting our obsessive micro-adjustments during this phase may be far less consequential than previously believed. Furthermore, our very understanding of sensory perception is being upended. Recent research indicates that the organic acids we so confidently claim to taste in a bright, complex pour-over are present in concentrations so low they border on the undetectable. We are no longer just brewing coffee; we are beginning to decode the cognitive illusions of flavor.\n\nThis scientific awakening coincides with a necessary geographic expansion of the specialty coffee map. As climate realities shift, the industry is looking beyond traditional equatorial strongholds. We are tracking the compelling resurgence of Sri Lanka—once a global coffee titan in the 1860s before being decimated by leaf rust—now strategically positioning itself to reclaim its heritage through meticulously processed specialty lots. Concurrently, the high-altitude, untapped microclimates of Nepal are drawing serious attention, promising entirely new terroirs and flavor profiles for the discerning palate.\n\nTranslating these macro-shifts from the farm and the laboratory to the cup requires both culinary innovation and precise equipment. On the hospitality front, institutions like The Caffè by Mr. Espresso are actively blurring the lines between gastronomy and cafe culture with highly curated seasonal pop-ups. Meanwhile, the democratization of precision extraction continues at home, with newly minted gear guides highlighting the increasingly sophisticated espresso technology now available for under $250.\n\nUltimately, the Fourth Wave is characterized by this relentless pursuit of clarity. By stripping away outdated sensory myths and expanding our agronomic borders, we continue to elevate coffee from a romanticized craft to a precise, relentlessly evolving science."};
window.EDITORIAL_HISTORY = [{"id":"ed_2026-09-24","publishDate":"2026-09-24","status":"published","title":"Shifts at the Source and the Pursuit of Extraction Perfection","dek":"","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laila-the-floor.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/another-barista-champion-just-competed-in-a-nationally-televised-game-show-1360538.html","wordCount":329,"paragraphs":["As one LA roaster radically rethinks the supply chain, we explore Nepal’s rising terroir, the extremes of bean-by-bean grinding, and a barista champion’s national TV debut.","The structural norms of specialty coffee's supply chain are undergoing significant shifts. Los Angeles specialty coffee company Go Get Em Tiger has launched a \"Roasted At Origin\" initiative, with plans to move 20% of its roasting operations directly to producing countries by the end of 2026, scaling to an ambitious 70% by the end of 2027. This push to retain value at the source parallels the continuous expansion of the global coffee map, highlighting compelling new origins like Nepal. While the landlocked nation lacks the established recognition of Colombia or Ethiopia, its rapid evolution and unique terroir make it an increasingly vital player in the specialty industry.","On the bar, the technical pursuit of the perfect cup continues—sometimes bordering on the absurd. A recent dive by Barista Hustle explored grinding coffee bean-by-bean, categorizing it alongside cryogenic grinding as a form of \"impractical perfection\" that is generally dismissed as inappropriate for commercial environments. A more practical pursuit of perfection can be found in the newest wave of high-extraction espresso filter baskets, where manufacturers have completely re-thought bottom hole patterns to ensure even extraction.","Manual brewing and travel gear are also seeing clever innovations. CoffeeGeek recently detailed a hack to build a fantastic, full-immersion, no-bypass brewer using two inexpensive Hario devices—the Mugen and the Switch. For brewers on the go, the new Outin Mino travel espresso maker promises a smaller footprint, though The Coffee Chronicler notes it represents two steps forward, but one step back where it really matters.","In community news, the organization Not a Barista is testing a new Shopify storefront to provide a smoother shopping experience and clearer product information for its supporters. Finally, specialty coffee continues its crossover into mainstream pop culture, with US Barista Champion Laila Ghambari recently appearing as a contestant on the nationally televised game show The Floor."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-23","publishDate":"2026-09-23","status":"published","title":"From Puck Prep to People Power","dek":"Today’s dispatch highlights the relentless science of extraction, the rise of capable travel espresso, and why the future of the industry belongs to its communities.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/tdg_bellwoodgrantpark_web-@grayson-guldenschuh-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-bellwood-coffee-in-atlanta-ga-2-1327933.html","wordCount":305,"paragraphs":["Specialty coffee has always been a beautiful tug-of-war between precise science and profound human connection. Today’s news perfectly captures that duality, proving that our industry is rapidly evolving on both fronts.","On the human side, the major headline comes from Cafe Imports, which has officially transitioned to a 100% employee-owned model via an ESOP. It’s a massive step forward for equitable business structures in the green coffee sector. Meanwhile, Not a Barista brings us the inspiring story of Bryant Toh, a social worker in Singapore brilliantly utilizing coffee as a bridge to build trust and support youth mental health. It’s a powerful reminder that cafes—like the stunning new Bellwood Coffee build-out in Atlanta recently spotlighted by Sprudge—are more than just retail spaces; they are vital community hubs. The Not a Barista team is also currently on the move, reporting from Dehua in the mountainous Fujian province, tracing the global threads that tie this community together at origin.","Back at the bar, the pursuit of extraction perfection continues unabated. Barista Hustle is digging deep into the physics of puck prep this week. They’re exploring how densification creates more spherical coffee particles for exceptionally even water flow, while also breaking down the ongoing mechanics of the RDT (Ross Droplet Technique) versus the AutoComb. It seems academic science is finally catching up to what home baristas have intuitively known about static reduction for two decades.","For those who refuse to leave this precision at home, The Coffee Chronicler takes a look at the booming travel espresso market. With thorough reviews of the flagship Mhw-3bomber Xpress-58 and the surprisingly capable MiiCoffee Eon (boasting a genuine 51mm basket), battery-operated espresso is proving it’s no longer a gimmick. Whether you're pulling shots in a high-end cafe, a youth center, or out of a backpack, the fourth wave continues to push boundaries."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-22","publishDate":"2026-09-22","status":"published","title":"Shaking the Grounds","dek":"Science, Stance, and Sanctuary","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/cascara-cabinet-rtd-01-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/ready-to-drink-the-cascara-fizz-boom-from-cascara-cabinet-1278625.html","wordCount":328,"paragraphs":["As extraction methods evolve and unexpected machines earn top marks, the coffee industry is finding its voice on global politics and celebrating the human stories behind the bar.","The specialty coffee community can no longer afford to exist in a vacuum. This reality is brought to the forefront today by CoffeeGeek’s impassioned editorial, which urges coffee professionals and enthusiasts alike to take a definitive stand against fascist policies reshaping our world. Coffee has always been inherently political, and as institutions like Ritual Coffee Roasters unveil their latest stunning build-out in San Francisco, we are reminded that our cafes are the very community hubs where these vital societal conversations take root.","On the bar, we are witnessing a rigorous, joyful re-examination of our mechanics. Barista Hustle explores Lance Hedrick’s recent revelation that simply shaking espresso grounds can dramatically boost extraction, challenging long-held distribution dogmas. They also delve into the potent legacy of the coffee overdose—the famed triple ristretto—with 2024 World Barista Champion Mikael Jasin. Even our gear expectations are being delightfully upended. CoffeeGeek's review of the $549 Ninja Luxe Café Premier proves that what some initially dismissed as a consumer toy is actually an 88.5-scoring powerhouse, proving innovation is becoming more accessible. And for those seeking a lighter refreshment amidst the heavy science, Cascara Cabinet is sparking a fizzy RTD cascara renaissance.","Yet, beneath the high-extraction science and political discourse, coffee remains intimately human. Not a Barista captures this ethos perfectly in two new profiles. They trace Lonika Tay’s inspiring ascent from weighing beans and sealing bags in an Indonesian roastery to commanding the international competition stage. We also get a glimpse into the beautiful chaos of Angel Kotsifakis’s coffee lab, where the intense focus of brewing is punctuated by the gleeful shouts of his child. Seeing a small bicycle nestled among gleaming espresso equipment is a poignant reminder: the Fourth Wave isn’t just about the perfect cup—it’s about honoring the real, complex lives of the people pouring them."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-21","publishDate":"2026-09-21","status":"published","title":"The Machine and the Bean","dek":"Where Coffee's Next Wave Is Breaking","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeechronicler.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Screenshot-2025-04-22-at-16.39.33.jpg","heroCredit":"Sour Espresso: Specialty Coffee’s Uncomfortable Truth","heroSource":"Coffee Chronicler","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeechronicler.com/why-is-my-espresso-sour/","wordCount":292,"paragraphs":["Coffee has always lived between two impulses—the human hand and the engineered system—and this week the tension is everywhere you look.","On the industry side, the machines are quietly taking over the back office. RoastConsole now offers to run an entire small roastery on a single platform, from roast log to invoice, priced by roasted volume with no add-ons. Smart Care, meanwhile, is rethinking how speed ovens and coffee equipment get serviced, betting on flat rates and faster turnaround. The plumbing of the trade is being rebuilt, and it favors those who can standardize.","But standardization has a shadow. From the origin side comes the harder story. Riza Zahrul Huda's biggest challenge in coffee turned out not to be pests or weather or quality, but something more structural—the quiet difficulty of getting a small village's harvest to a market that will value it. And CoffeeGeek's \"Hollow Harvest\" names the pressure directly: fifty-percent tariffs, a creeping Robusta substitution, and a favorite Brazilian suddenly tasting woody and costing seven dollars more. The cup in your hand is a ledger of forces most drinkers never see.","Which is why the craft still matters. The Coffee Chronicler reminds us that dialing in isn't a cure-all—persistent sourness in espresso points to deeper problems no grind adjustment will fix. Coffee Ad Astra keeps chasing the physics, restraining flow to tame channeling with something as humble as a pasta-strainer mesh. And in Shenzhen, the team behind MOMENTEM spent January doing nothing but testing a grinder, refusing to rush, insisting it behave exactly as they believed it should.","That is the Fourth Wave in miniature: precision engineering in service of an agricultural act, systems and soil pulling against each other—and the best of us refusing to let go of either."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-20","publishDate":"2026-09-20","status":"published","title":"The first lot from Vietnam","dek":"Thirty years. Screening that still has to happen.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Grounds-for-Health-auction.png","heroCredit":"Grounds for Health auction. Daily Coffee News.","heroSource":"Daily Coffee News","heroSourceUrl":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/16/grounds-for-health-marks-30-years-with-annual-coffee-auction/","wordCount":294,"paragraphs":["Grounds for Health turns thirty this year. The programs sit in Ethiopia and Kenya. More than 265,000 women have been screened for cervical cancer. More than 22,000 have had preventive treatment. The green coffee auction that funds that work opens Wednesday. Crop to Cup sent the first Vietnam lot the auction has ever listed. Great Cherry, in Dak Lak. An anaerobic natural robusta. One hundred thirty two pounds. Cupped at 86. Opening at 5.75 a pound. Justin Mool says the auction is how the industry keeps the connection going.","On Sunday, September 20, 2026, we hold that first lot to one pressure: whether the cup still pays for care at origin when a new room in San Francisco imports Europe, and when the drinker is still a guess.","SPRO Coffee Lab opened at 201 Spear Street, a few blocks from the Ferry Building. European style. Coffees from Barcelona, Copenhagen, Amsterdam, Paris. Modbar. A Weber EG-1 for frozen coffee. Liza Otanes, with her husband, also built The Wild Fox. Japanese roasting partners. A Cup of Excellence Brazil at 105 dollars a cup. Cheryl Hung and Brian Gaffney launched Furthers and Betters because the industry knows the product and guesses at the person who drinks it. After the 7.4 earthquake near San José del Palmar, Jimmy Butler's Bigface and J Balvin put a 78 dollar bundle on El Capitán, net to Colombia Se Levanta. Houthis took Mokha, the port that named mocha. La Cimbali's Supera is rated for 350 cups a day. Sprudge's Roaster's Village would pick them all.","A first lot from Vietnam. Thirty years of screens. The fourth wave is not the auction catalog. It is whether the cup still pays for the woman at origin, and whether the room still names the farm."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-19","publishDate":"2026-09-19","status":"published","title":"The Craft and the Convenience","dek":"","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/green-room-sprudge-lede.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/checking-in-on-the-other-side-of-the-supply-chain-with-the-green-room-1349454.html","wordCount":299,"paragraphs":["There's a quiet tension running through coffee right now, and this week's stories draw it out from four directions.","On one side sits convenience. App ordering and drive-thrus are reshaping how most people actually buy their coffee, and the shift is a double-edged sword: faster service and frictionless payment on one hand, the slow erosion of the counter conversation on the other. When the transaction disappears into a phone, something of the ritual goes with it.","On the other side sits craft, and it's alive and well among the people who care about extraction down to the compound. The obsessives are still asking hard questions — how astringency actually gets pulled from the bean, how to prep an espresso puck for a more even shot. This is coffee as a problem worth solving, not just a drink to grab. The gear reflects it too: the Flair 58 remains a serious manual machine years on, wired up with thermocouples to prove it, while newer gadgets like the Outin Claro learn that a clever form factor isn't the same as a useful tool.","Zoom out and the human supply chain comes into focus. A look behind the green-coffee curtain reminds us how many hands touch a cup before we do, and voices from origin — a barista's journey out of lockdown in Malaysia, remote internships built around sustainability and community — insist that coffee's future is a shared, global project, not just a Western café aesthetic.","That's the throughline. The frictionless future is coming fast, and it's genuinely convenient. But the craft, the science, and the people who grow and pour it are what keep coffee worth caring about. The best cafés won't choose between the two. They'll find a way to serve the app order and still remember your name."],"body":"There's a quiet tension running through coffee right now, and this week's stories draw it out from four directions.\n\nOn one side sits convenience. App ordering and drive-thrus are reshaping how most people actually buy their coffee, and the shift is a double-edged sword: faster service and frictionless payment on one hand, the slow erosion of the counter conversation on the other. When the transaction disappears into a phone, something of the ritual goes with it.\n\nOn the other side sits craft, and it's alive and well among the people who care about extraction down to the compound. The obsessives are still asking hard questions — how astringency actually gets pulled from the bean, how to prep an espresso puck for a more even shot. This is coffee as a problem worth solving, not just a drink to grab. The gear reflects it too: the Flair 58 remains a serious manual machine years on, wired up with thermocouples to prove it, while newer gadgets like the Outin Claro learn that a clever form factor isn't the same as a useful tool.\n\nZoom out and the human supply chain comes into focus. A look behind the green-coffee curtain reminds us how many hands touch a cup before we do, and voices from origin — a barista's journey out of lockdown in Malaysia, remote internships built around sustainability and community — insist that coffee's future is a shared, global project, not just a Western café aesthetic.\n\nThat's the throughline. The frictionless future is coming fast, and it's genuinely convenient. But the craft, the science, and the people who grow and pour it are what keep coffee worth caring about. The best cafés won't choose between the two. They'll find a way to serve the app order and still remember your name."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-18","publishDate":"2026-09-18","status":"published","title":"He pointed at the board","dek":"Fourteen. A pour-over instead.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://notabarista.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Jimmy-Adame-1024x576.jpg","heroCredit":"Jimmy Adame. Not a Barista.","heroSource":"Not a Barista","heroSourceUrl":"https://notabarista.org/jimmy-adame/","wordCount":281,"paragraphs":["A 14-year-old kid at a Michigan farmer's market. Flannel and Neff beanies. He points at the board. He thinks he is ordering a blackberry-milk-chocolate latte. The barista hands him a pour-over instead. Jimmy Adame. Not a Barista tells the rest as coffee, chaos, and second acts. The board had a name. The cup had a farm. He stayed long enough for the second act to start.","On Friday, September 18, 2026, we hold that pour-over to one pressure: whether the cup still arrives as what the farm grew, not as the name on the board, when a third group is already scrap, and when a label says direct.","Stumptown is in Los Angeles. A flagship in the historic Lincoln Heights Theatre. Motors Coffee of Paris takes over Sey in Bushwick this weekend. Paris in Brooklyn for a few days. Barista Hustle says the resale on a one-group high-end machine is staggering. A three-group is terrible. The industry does not value the third group. A second-hand Linea opened first cafes. The MVP is newer and costs more. Coffee Chronicler put the Kingrinder K7 on the bench. It tries a lot. It almost succeeds. Except where it is most crucial. CoffeeGeek asks what direct trade means for the farmer who grew the cup. Direct. Fair. Commodity. Three labels. Three promises. They are not the same. Edward Choo did not grow up loving coffee. In Malaysia the drink was Milo. At the kopitiam he stole a sip. Bitter. Intense. Not something he wanted.","A pour-over instead of the board. Milo first. Then a sip. The fourth wave is not the third group. It is whether someone still hands you the cup the farm made."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-17","publishDate":"2026-09-17","status":"published","title":"The baristas at the center","dek":"Helen Diller. A long curved bar.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sightglass-berkeley-4.jpg","heroCredit":"Sightglass Coffee, Berkeley. Sprudge.","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-sightglass-coffee-in-berkeley-ca-1275259.html","wordCount":283,"paragraphs":["Sightglass opened its fifth cafe on May 8, 2026, on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House, where downtown meets campus. Lundberg Design started from filtration and percolation, and from a vintage Probat. Concentric circles from the logo organize the plan. A long curved bar puts the baristas, and the work of making coffee, at the center of the room. Overhead, a soffit takes the shape of a bean hopper. A drum of steel mesh filters the daylight.","On Thursday, September 17, 2026, we hold that bar to one pressure: whether the work of making coffee is still allowed to sit in the middle of the room when the cone is called out of control, and when decaf costs the same as the good stuff.","The Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named Café Baluarte de Veracruz overall. Producers sit on the same card. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy, an out of control system. The UX method tries to separate the variables with a lid and a magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint-stripper headline. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle: no stickers on the espresso machine. Jane Cabusas Espante still chose the smaller cezve stage. Not a Barista, quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.","A curved bar. Baristas in the middle. The fourth wave is not a louder championship. It is whether the work still has the center of the room."],"body":"Sightglass opened its fifth cafe on May 8, 2026, on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House, where downtown meets campus. Lundberg Design started from filtration and percolation, and from a vintage Probat. Concentric circles from the logo organize the plan. A long curved bar puts the baristas, and the work of making coffee, at the center of the room. Overhead, a soffit takes the shape of a bean hopper. A drum of steel mesh filters the daylight.\n\nOn Thursday, September 17, 2026, we hold that bar to one pressure: whether the work of making coffee is still allowed to sit in the middle of the room when the cone is called out of control, and when decaf costs the same as the good stuff.\n\nThe Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named Café Baluarte de Veracruz overall. Producers sit on the same card. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy, an out of control system. The UX method tries to separate the variables with a lid and a magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint-stripper headline. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle: no stickers on the espresso machine. Jane Cabusas Espante still chose the smaller cezve stage. Not a Barista, quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.\n\nA curved bar. Baristas in the middle. The fourth wave is not a louder championship. It is whether the work still has the center of the room."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-16","publishDate":"2026-09-16","status":"published","title":"She chose the smaller stage","dek":"A cezve that still counts. Work at the center of the room.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://notabarista.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Jane-1-scaled.jpg","heroCredit":"Jane Cabusas Espante. Not a Barista.","heroSource":"Not a Barista","heroSourceUrl":"https://notabarista.org/%f0%9d%96%a9%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%97%87%f0%9d%96%be-%f0%9d%96%a2%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%96%bb%f0%9d%97%8e%f0%9d%97%8c%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%97%8c-%f0%9d%96%a4%f0%9d%97%8c%f0%9d%97%89%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%97%87/","wordCount":319,"paragraphs":["Jane Cabusas Espante chose the smaller stage. Cezve. Ibrik. Next to the Barista Championship or Brewers Cup, the pot rarely draws the same room. The return is uncertain. Sometimes the contest does not happen at all. She went anyway. The traditional brew. A story. A line back to the root. In Dubai she won the 2026 World Cezve/Ibrik Championship for the United Arab Emirates. A carbonic macerated Colombia Gesha with a natural Panama Gesha. Cryoconcentrate. Strawberries. Angel Kotsifakis second. Wael Hachem third. Not a Barista says the return was not measured in exposure.","On Wednesday, September 16, 2026, we hold that smaller stage to one pressure: whether the oldest brew still has a room when the cone is called out of control, and when a new cafe puts the work in the middle.","Sightglass opened its fifth cafe on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House. Lundberg Design started from filtration and a vintage Probat. A long curved bar. The baristas at the center. The Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named the region's best roasters and the producers behind those bags. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy. An out of control system. Their UX method tries to separate the variables. A magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint stripper ban. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle says do not put stickers on the espresso machine. I'M NOT A BARISTA has been quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.","A smaller stage. Work at the center of the room. The fourth wave is not the louder championship. It is whether someone still chooses the pot that has to be kept."],"body":"Jane Cabusas Espante chose the smaller stage. Cezve. Ibrik. Next to the Barista Championship or Brewers Cup, the pot rarely draws the same room. The return is uncertain. Sometimes the contest does not happen at all. She went anyway. The traditional brew. A story. A line back to the root. In Dubai she won the 2026 World Cezve/Ibrik Championship for the United Arab Emirates. A carbonic macerated Colombia Gesha with a natural Panama Gesha. Cryoconcentrate. Strawberries. Angel Kotsifakis second. Wael Hachem third. Not a Barista says the return was not measured in exposure.\n\nOn Wednesday, September 16, 2026, we hold that smaller stage to one pressure: whether the oldest brew still has a room when the cone is called out of control, and when a new cafe puts the work in the middle.\n\nSightglass opened its fifth cafe on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House. Lundberg Design started from filtration and a vintage Probat. A long curved bar. The baristas at the center. The Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named the region's best roasters and the producers behind those bags. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy. An out of control system. Their UX method tries to separate the variables. A magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint stripper ban. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle says do not put stickers on the espresso machine. I'M NOT A BARISTA has been quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.\n\nA smaller stage. Work at the center of the room. The fourth wave is not the louder championship. It is whether someone still chooses the pot that has to be kept."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-15","publishDate":"2026-09-15","status":"published","title":"Named for the world","dek":"Buon Ma Thuot. Farms that held.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/coffee_museum_coffee_harvesting_artifacts_exhibition_area_world_coffee_museum_07.jpg","heroCredit":"World Coffee Museum, Buon Ma Thuot. Sprudge.","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/in-vietnam-the-world-coffee-museum-tells-a-global-story-1250720.html","wordCount":289,"paragraphs":["In Buon Ma Thuot, in Vietnam’s coffee highlands, the World Coffee Museum tells a global story. Not a Vietnam museum. World. Jens Burg spent two decades collecting. More than 10,000 artifacts moved from Hamburg after Dang Le Nguyen Vu of Trung Nguyen acquired the collection. The building takes the longhouse of the Ede. Coffee as shared heritage, from a producing nation.","On Tuesday, September 15, 2026, we hold that naming to one pressure: whether the cup still belongs to the farm when the season is sold in a box, and when a pitcher has to be split so both drinks get the foam.","Puerto Rico was once the world’s seventh largest producer. About 2,000 farms now. Irma and Maria took up to 90 percent of the plants. They held. Jamaica’s Blue Mountain is less than 0.01 percent of world supply. About 80 percent of those farms sit under five acres. Japan still takes most of it. Sprudge says pumpkin spice is dead. Titan Caskets sells an orange box for sixteen hundred dollars. PSL season does not have to end when you do. Barista Hustle teaches how to split a pitcher so both drinks get the same foam. Four quarters. Pour three, return one. Nutation packs the puck denser. Coarse particles fall. Some of those boulders may only have been clumps. CoffeeGeek says the grinder is still the first tool. Twelve budget picks. A Kingrinder at 45 dollars. An Encore that will not retire. Moderate coffee, three to four cups, still looks after the brain and the heart.","A museum named for the world. Farms that held. The fourth wave is not the casket for the season. It is whether the cup still belongs to the farm that grew it."],"body":"In Buon Ma Thuot, in Vietnam’s coffee highlands, the World Coffee Museum tells a global story. Not a Vietnam museum. World. Jens Burg spent two decades collecting. More than 10,000 artifacts moved from Hamburg after Dang Le Nguyen Vu of Trung Nguyen acquired the collection. The building takes the longhouse of the Ede. Coffee as shared heritage, from a producing nation.\n\nOn Tuesday, September 15, 2026, we hold that naming to one pressure: whether the cup still belongs to the farm when the season is sold in a box, and when a pitcher has to be split so both drinks get the foam.\n\nPuerto Rico was once the world’s seventh largest producer. About 2,000 farms now. Irma and Maria took up to 90 percent of the plants. They held. Jamaica’s Blue Mountain is less than 0.01 percent of world supply. About 80 percent of those farms sit under five acres. Japan still takes most of it. Sprudge says pumpkin spice is dead. Titan Caskets sells an orange box for sixteen hundred dollars. PSL season does not have to end when you do. Barista Hustle teaches how to split a pitcher so both drinks get the same foam. Four quarters. Pour three, return one. Nutation packs the puck denser. Coarse particles fall. Some of those boulders may only have been clumps. CoffeeGeek says the grinder is still the first tool. Twelve budget picks. A Kingrinder at 45 dollars. An Encore that will not retire. Moderate coffee, three to four cups, still looks after the brain and the heart.\n\nA museum named for the world. Farms that held. The fourth wave is not the casket for the season. It is whether the cup still belongs to the farm that grew it."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-14","publishDate":"2026-09-14","status":"published","title":"The question stayed","dek":"Cycling before school. Beans falling through a hole in Boise","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/slow-by-slow-11.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-slow-by-slow-in-boise-id-1274400.html","wordCount":315,"paragraphs":["Kay Cheon’s coffee began with a question that did not go away. He cycled before school. Coffee was just there. Then friends handed him cups that were not merely better. Different. He needed to know why. A V60. A shop in Los Angeles in 2013. The stage in 2018 to learn, not to win. In 2025 he became United States Barista Champion. He says the community is what kept him. Curiosity and flavor would not have been enough. In Santa Barbara he is building Parterre. A garden. Nature, arranged.","On Monday, September 15, 2026, we hold that question to one pressure: whether the cup still answers when the brew is slow on purpose, and when the beans fall through a hole in the bar.","Not a Barista went to Chongqing for ICCF First as friends. The room was packed. Four people stood around one brew, passed it, laughed. Coffee felt close again. Sprudge walked Slow by Slow in Boise. Ten years. Fast by Slow opened next door on July 17. Same coffee, faster. Under the bar a Weber EG-1 eats beans through a hand-carved vortex. You watch them disappear. Accademia del Caffè Espresso in Florence is dropping Coffee Facts. Sixteen topics. Four chapters. Seed to cup. Jonathan Gagné read 300 particle size distributions on 24 espresso grinders. Dialing in is dialing in the fines. He got the Pulsar. A valve. No bypass. A centimeter of slurry. Coffee Review cupped nearly 200 darker roasts. The top ten sat Agtron 44 to 47, the cusp of second crack. Mark Prince put cà phê sữa đá back. A phin. Four to five minutes. Condensed milk is structural. The slow drip is the point.","A question that stayed. A packed room in Chongqing. Beans falling through a hole. The fourth wave is not the chapter count. It is whether someone still stays long enough for the cup to answer."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-13","publishDate":"2026-09-13","status":"published","title":"Already there","dek":"Coffee since six, a new room in Copenhagen.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/8th-roast-kilimbi-anaerobic-sprudge-roasters-village-copy.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/a-fresh-roster-of-fresh-coffees-from-the-sprudge-roasters-village-1332178.html","wordCount":293,"paragraphs":["For Andrés Paparoni, coffee was never something he found later. Growing up in Venezuela, it was family. His father, Gustavo, a Q grader and teacher. His grandfather sold Gaggia. He drank it from six. At fourteen he was behind the bar with his father. Not every branch of the family stayed in coffee. Enough of it stayed to shape the room he grew up in. A Gardelli Panama and a Sasa Sestic routine made him want to train, compete, stay. He is now a Spanish barista champion, instructing at Nomad in Barcelona. Origin was not a tasting note. It was the house.","On Sunday, September 13, 2026, we hold that house to one pressure: who the cup is for when the bag is fresh from a village, and when the scale does every job.","Sprudge restocked the Roaster's Village. A weekly list because people keep asking where to buy. Sprudge put Fresh Pots in Copenhagen on the Build-Outs. A new cafe. A new counter. CoffeeGeek put a Laybird MagAttach on the bench. Sixty nine dollars. Magnets. One tiny scale for espresso and pour over, no app, a live ratio in red. Option-O's Lagom Casa is the other all rounder: slow conical burrs, a magnetic cup, a knocker, espresso and filter without a second machine. Jonathan Gagné's Physics of Espresso is out, graphs and drawings, sold through Scott Rao. I'M NOT A BARISTA took charity wristbands, L1GHT cups, and a MOMENTEM grinder to Bangkok. Not only to show the work.","A life that was already coffee. A bag that arrived this week. A room that just opened. The fourth wave is not the scale that does many jobs. It is whether the cup still belongs to the house that had it from the start."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-12","publishDate":"2026-09-12","status":"published","title":"It began with people","dek":"Tay in the first year. A pouring arm on autopilot.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeegeek.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/xBloom-Studio-Hero-1.jpg","heroCredit":"xBloom Studio. CoffeeGeek.","heroSource":"CoffeeGeek","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeegeek.com/blog/new-products/xbloom-studio-competition-level-pour-over-on-autopilot/","wordCount":302,"paragraphs":["For Tay Wipvasutt, coffee began with people. Still in his first year of university, he took a part time job at a coffee shop. Not a flavor first. Not a machine. Not a scoresheet. People. Simon Sun, as a child, lost a dog that had lived with the family four or five years. What stayed was not only the loss. He never knew what happened. If it had died, he thought, at least he would have known.","On Saturday, September 12, 2026, we hold that first job to one pressure: who the cup is for when the arm pours itself, and when the cezve is faster than the spro.","You are at home. You want coffee. You are already going out the door. Barista Hustle says it is not espresso. The cezve is mightier than the spro when time is the only brief. Another piece splits the bar: some steam one drink at a time, some steam in twos. One is not better. Latte art champions tend to steam for two. Sprudge gathered more gadgets this week. Gearly beloved. Perfect Daily Grind recapped the rest: Global Coffee Awards for US and Canada roasters, a Uganda and South Korea deal, Luckin taking a billion dollar stake story, Joe Coffee and Cometeer with JetBlue. Coffee Chronicler put the cup last: ceramic or glass as the final piece of the flavor puzzle. CoffeeGeek put the xBloom Studio on autopilot. Whole bean to cup. Grinder, scale, kettle, an oscillating pouring arm. Competition level pour over without a hand on the kettle.","A first year behind the bar. A dog that did not come back. An arm that pours. The fourth wave is not the gadget of the week. It is whether the cup still begins with people when the kettle has learned to move by itself."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-11","publishDate":"2026-09-11","status":"published","title":"She walked into the room","dek":"Noon at twenty seven. Extra hot is not extra good.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://notabarista.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/noon-1024x576.jpg","heroCredit":"Noon Nutrada Kunavivattananon. Not a Barista.","heroSource":"Not a Barista","heroSourceUrl":"https://notabarista.org/noon-nutrada-kunavivattananon/","wordCount":285,"paragraphs":["Noon Nutrada Kunavivattananon walked into a room at twenty seven as CEO of one of Thailand's largest specialty coffee companies. A few years later she walked into another as the first female president of the Specialty Coffee Association of Thailand. Not a Barista tells that walk. The same week the studio paused most operations for family. The 135 ml L1GHT cups already packed still went out.","On Friday, September 11, 2026, we hold that walk to one pressure: who still sits with the cup when extra hot is the risk, and when the shot is already in the fridge.","Sprudge says extra hot coffee is not extra good for the throat. Esophageal cancer risk rises with temperature, not with the bean. Extra hot is a temperature, not a roast. Blanchard's Coffee put Ninety Three Million on a bag. Design as the name on the door. Barista Hustle cupped prebatched espresso against fresh. A podcast voice said we should be hyping this more. Another piece counts World Barista Championship time: twelve drinks in fifteen minutes. Seventy five seconds a drink. The title counts two hundred ten. The clock is the routine. Coffee Chronicler put the DeLonghi Essenza Mini on the bench. Heat in twenty five seconds. Width of a hand. Hot shots. Nice crema. Not really espresso. Easy mode. The same desk still ranks drip: Moccamaster, Aiden, Breville Precision. Peak automatic. The question is which one you live with.","A woman in the room. A bag with a number. A shot that waited. The fourth wave is not the machine that heats in half a minute. It is whether someone still walks in and sits down with the cup at a temperature a throat can keep."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-09","publishDate":"2026-09-09","status":"published","title":"Not just the judges","dek":"Champions on the road. Milk that takes on water from the wand","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://perfectdailygrind.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Boram-Um.webp","heroCredit":"Why the world’s best baristas are going on tour","heroSource":"Perfect Daily Grind","heroSourceUrl":"https://perfectdailygrind.com/2026/09/baristas-going-on-tour/","wordCount":295,"paragraphs":["Champion baristas are going on tour. Bar takeovers. Tastings. A walk through the competition routine for people who never sat with the judges. Jack Simpson, 2025 World Barista Champion, is with FAEMA in Asia Pacific. On 14 September he is in Barcelona for a tasting and a live Q and A. Boram Um took the same road after 2023, across North America, Europe, and Asia. The events rarely turn a profit. The title still has to meet the room.","On Friday, September 11, 2026, we hold that room to one pressure: who still pours for the people who drink it when the milk is a bot, and when the crema is made after the shot.","The Global Coffee Awards named Sweet Bloom Coffee of Denver overall winner for the United States and Canada. Houston, 10 and 11 August. Double blind. Q graders. Barista Hustle weighed steamed milk at 55 C and found 7.5 to 10 percent extra water from the wand. Jack Simpson and Anthony Douglas concentrate milk instead. Automated foamers do not add that water. Ubermilk Plus can tap alternative milks. A later blind test of fake crema, a Nanofoamer on premade espresso, went 9 to 1 against the fake. The fake was more acrid. Asser Christensen unpacked a grinder in Denmark and brewed a coffee he knew. It tasted like nothing. Fruit gone. Danish tap. Filter coffee, he says, wants water between 30 and 100 TDS. The Breville Bambino Plus still pulls shots he likes, years on. A beginner machine.","A champion in a room that is not a stage. A wand that waters the milk. A cup that went quiet. The fourth wave is not the trophy. It is whether the pour still happens in front of the people who drink it."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-08","publishDate":"2026-09-08","status":"published","title":"Not a statement cafe","dek":"Two dollars in Over-the-Rhine. A beach already had the brief.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Radical-Bean-Cincinatti-Ohio-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Radical Bean Cooperative, Over-the-Rhine. Daily Coffee News.","heroSource":"Daily Coffee News","heroSourceUrl":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/08/radical-bean-wants-to-rework-the-coffee-model-in-cincinnati/","wordCount":294,"paragraphs":["Radical Bean Cooperative reopened in the Volkshaus, northern Over-the-Rhine, after Topia shut for two months and nearly stayed shut. Rayan Saoud is the sole worker-owner. Drip is two dollars. Urbana roasts. Lebanese pastry from Dean's Mediterranean. The boutique pourover shop is gone. The neighbors are not.","On Tuesday, September 8, 2026, we hold those two dollars to one pressure: whether the room still belongs to the street that walks past it.","Eventide Coffee opened three thousand square feet near the sea in Chennai. Billboards, the architects, said the brief was never a statement cafe. Calm. The beach already had everything they needed. Ibrahim H. Al Mallouhi left a vice president's chair to chase a smell from a grandmother roasting on the floor near the port. He wants to be remembered for serving the world's best coffee, not the title. Origin in the hand. Then a shop that can hold it without performing.","Barista Hustle watched World Brewers Cup finalists updose to an average 1:14.1. Classic third-wave ratios sat at 1:16 to 1:17. The scoresheet changed. The street did not. The spent puck is still being asked to become a filter for the next shot. Mark Prince put cordless espresso grinders on the bench. USB-C will jump a diesel in winter. It will not, yet, hold torque through a dense light roast. Hand grinding 18.5 grams took forty-five seconds. The motor took longer. A leftover is a fact. A score is a fashion. A battery is a promise.","A cooperative on McMicken. A curved counter facing the Madras beach. A crank that does not sag. The fourth wave is not a new brand. It is whether Cincinnati and Chennai are still in the same sentence: the room belongs as long as they will have us."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-07","publishDate":"2026-09-07","status":"published","title":"The crank still turns","dek":"A tenth shop in Greenpoint. A motor that stalls on a dense bean.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/variety-coffee-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Variety Coffee Roasters, Greenpoint. Sprudge Build-Outs of Coffee.","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-variety-coffee-roasters-in-brooklyn-ny-1265757.html","wordCount":286,"paragraphs":["Variety Coffee Roasters opened its tenth cafe in Greenpoint, back where the company started. Nearly twenty years. Queens. Manhattan. Still independent. No outside investor. Sprudge walked the new room, and posted this week's Roaster's Village list besides.","On Sunday, September 6, 2026, we hold that room to one pressure: what still turns when you unplug the machine, and who still owns the key.","Ibrahim H. Al Mallouhi left a vice president's chair in telecom to chase a smell he first met as a boy in an old house near the port, his grandmother roasting on the floor. He wants to be remembered for serving the best coffee the world produces. Not the biggest title. He found specialty coffee in a New York cafe called Fika. Then he built The Espresso Lab. The fourth wave is that arc: origin in the hand, then a shop that can hold it.","Barista Hustle asks if World Brewers Cup scoring for taste descriptors is inflating brew ratios — more water, more theater. The spent puck is still being asked to become compost, board, filter. A leftover is a fact. A score is a fashion.","Mark Prince put cordless espresso grinders on the bench. USB-C will jump a diesel in winter. It will not, yet, hold torque through a dense light roast at 300 microns. A screwdriver on a Kingrinder stalls. Hand grinding 18.5 grams took 45 seconds. The motor took longer, then the shot choked on fines. The arm is still the flywheel.","A tenth cafe without a fund. A grandmother's pan. A crank that does not sag. The fourth wave is not a new battery. It is whether Greenpoint and the port house are still in the same sentence."]}];
